2186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n¬(Y∧X)⇔Y≡[¬(Y∧X)⇒Y]∧\n$$\n\nWhat expression should be inserted into the blank space above to give logically equivalent statements?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "22a767fa-9117-5005-b0eb-ffcf7c23df74" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411154 #edb/ref 1074 "According to the definition of the biconditional, \n\n- $A⇔B$ is true if $A⇒B$ **and** $B⇒A.$ Otherwise, it is false.\n\n- In other words, $A⇔B$ is true if $A$ and $B$ have the same truth values. Otherwise, it is false.\n\nSo, in this case, we have\n\n$$\n¬(Y∧X)⇔Y≡[¬(Y∧X)⇒Y]∧[\\,Y⇒¬(Y∧X)].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing part is $Y⇒¬(Y∧X).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107158" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1071 "$¬A⇔B≡$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "75186b51-1b0d-58e4-a5b9-b90965af20bd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411155 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬A⇔B≡(¬A⇒B)∧(B⇒¬A)\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n(¬A⇒B)∧(B⇒¬A)≡(¬¬A∨B)∧(¬B∨¬A)\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∨B)∧(¬B∨¬A)≡(¬¬A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n(¬¬A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)≡(A∨B)∧¬(B∧A)\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182523" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1071 "$¬(Y⇔X)≡$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96aebafe-ff76-58ac-a04c-e47b82d4e2a3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186411157 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we use the definition of the biconditional statement to write the equivalence as a conjunction of implications: \n\n$$\n¬(Y⇔X)≡¬[(Y⇒X)∧(X⇒Y)]\n$$\n\nNow, we represent all the implications using disjunctions and negations:\n\n$$\n¬[(Y⇒X)∧(X⇒Y)]≡¬[(¬Y∨X)∧(¬X∨Y)]\n$$\n\nNext, we use De Morgan's laws:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬[(¬Y∨X)∧(¬X∨Y)] & ≡¬(¬Y∨X)∨¬(¬X∨Y) \\\\  & ≡(¬¬Y∧¬X)∨(¬¬X∧¬Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the double negations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬¬Y∧¬X)∨(¬¬X∧¬Y) & ≡(Y∧¬X)∨(X∧¬Y)\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412665 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412665 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412665 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "06c6a545-c229-5f5d-870c-b79d9c9a59c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412666 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412666 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412666 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4ca4de5e-af8a-5c24-a605-1616e6c252f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412667 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "58008e9d-9ba3-52c4-b5d8-03856d7c1035" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412668 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{F}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412668 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412668 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "740c5711-9a52-5628-87bd-76d9edb34cd1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412669 #edb/ref 1080 "\\text{T}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412669 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186412669 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "915ce5ec-5f08-5363-b3ad-6cb09fdb8977" #edb/ref 175921